Dear students,

Welcome to Math 163.

This is an ASYNCHRONOUS course. Therefore, there are NO LECTURES via zoom. =
It is best thought of as a new incarnation of what used to be called a 'Cor=
respondence Course'. Rather than receiving material by mail, one has access=
 to an online platform that houses the material. There will, however, be tu=
toring by dedicated tutors. At the moment I do not know who will tutor what=
 at what times. I will make an announcement once these decisions are made. =
I shall communicate with students SOLELY within CANVAS using EMAIL and ANNO=
UNCEMENTS. One must always read the announcements very carefully. Otherwise=
, you may miss various things.

VERY IMPORTANT

This is a SUMMER class. It is an ACCELERATED, 7-WEEK COURSE. It is running =
at DOUBLE THE NORMAL PACE. It is a mistake to think that one can assimilate=
 the material at double one's normal rate. Nevertheless, there are very cap=
able, dedicated, and well-prepared students who have secured a grade of A i=
n a summer class in the past. The time one needs to devote to this class is=
 at the very least 25 HOURS PER WEEK. I should estimate the time per week t=
o be between 25 HOURS and 40 HOURS.  Unless one can commit this amount of t=
ime, one should NOT take this class. In fact, if one has the choice, one sh=
ould take the class during a regular 14-week session. My students tell me t=
hat if one takes this class during the summer, one must understand that one=
 will have no life at all during these seven weeks.

VERY IMPORTANT

There is an exam every week and a problem-set due at the end of the term. O=
ne must immediately download the exams and start reading the notes slowly a=
nd carefully. One's goal is to finish at least the a and b problems and sub=
mit them by the due date. There is no textbook for this class. The material=
 that one needs to read appears under Files. All work MUST be done exactly =
as in the Notes with no changes, no variations, and no omissions. In case t=
here are any changes, variations, or omissions, points will be deducted. No=
 homework will be collected or graded. The course grade will be based entir=
ely on the performance on six take-home exams, and a Problem Set. Please re=
fer to the General Information Package under Files.

COLLABORATION

One is free to talk to anyone. But one must present one's answers in the ma=
nner in which they are presented in the Notes or Solved Exams with no excep=
tions whatsoever. Some tutors have very kindly made their old exams availab=
le to students. Some students copied blindly from these exams in the past a=
nd got ZEROES because they could not even copy faithfully and did not under=
stand what they needed to change. They also adopted conventions that the cu=
rrent notes do not have and that are, therefore, not allowed. These exams a=
re NEW and often differ from previous exams very slightly but such slight c=
hanges may make profound differences in the answers. Students have individu=
al exams that are made up from their J-numbers. Tutors will be able to read=
 the questions in English but may not be familiar with the conventions adop=
ted in the notes because the notes did not exist in this form when they too=
k the course. Every problem that one will be asked to solve has an analogue=
 in relevant notes. One must immediately start reading these documents as t=
hey contain exactly what I would have said in class and they proceed at a l=
eisurely pace. ONE MUST READ THE NOTES. Math 163 gets easier as the course =
progresses.

TUTORS

Dedicated tutors will be added as observers to this course. They have all t=
aken many of my classes and have been tutors for a long time for my classes=
. Their presence and participation are crucial to the life of this course a=
nd they will all help the current students.

DISCORD GROUPS

A group will be set up for each section on DISCORD and one should use these=
 groups as one's discussion forum as the current structure of CANVAS does n=
ot allow tutors to have the required access. Currently they can observe but=
 they cannot participate. But one may contact them within CANVAS. It may be=
 easier to contact them on DISCORD.



To make individual appointments or to join study groups, please visit Tutor=
ing and Study Groups under Files.  These will be updated once new arrangeme=
nts are announced. You may go to only these tutors and no one else.

For questions regarding tutoring, please write to Professor John Politis at=
 jpolitis@ccp.edu.



EVERY student needs to learn the language of sets and mappings because all =
discourse will be carried out in this language. This knowledge is required,=
 expected, and assumed for all courses. This material is taught in many cou=
rses including Discrete Mathematics (Math 163). Therefore, even if it is no=
t required, it is a very good idea to take Discrete Mathematics. Notes on t=
hese topics have been uploaded.

Please find out how to register for study groups or make individual appoint=
ments by sending an email to tutors within CANVAS or by writing to Professo=
r Politis.



Good luck.



