<p>Dear students,</p>
<p>Welcome to Math 163.</p>
<p>This is an&nbsp;<strong>ASYNCHRONOUS</strong>&nbsp;course.&nbsp;<strong>=
Therefore, there are NO LECTURES via zoom</strong>. It is best thought of a=
s a new incarnation of what used to be called a 'Correspondence Course'. Ra=
ther than receiving material by mail, one has access
 to an online platform that houses the material. There will, however, be tu=
toring by dedicated tutors. At the moment I do not know who will tutor what=
 at what times. I will make an announcement once these decisions are made.<=
span>&nbsp;</span><strong>I shall communicate
 with students SOLELY within CANVAS using EMAIL and ANNOUNCEMENTS. One must=
 always read the announcements very carefully. Otherwise, you may miss vari=
ous things</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong><span>VERY IMPORTANT</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>This is a&nbsp;<strong>SUMMER&nbsp;</strong>class<strong>.&nbsp;</strong=
>It is an<strong>&nbsp;ACCELERATED, 7-WEEK COURSE. It is running at DOUBLE =
THE NORMAL PACE.&nbsp;</strong>It is a mistake to think that<strong>&nbsp;o=
ne can assimilate the material at double one's normal rate.&nbsp;</strong>N=
evertheless,
 there are very capable, dedicated, and well-prepared students who have sec=
ured a grade of A in a summer class in the past. The time one needs to devo=
te to this class is at the very least<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>25 HOURS PE=
R WEEK</strong>. I should estimate the
 time per week to be between&nbsp;<strong>25 HOURS and 40 HOURS.&nbsp;</str=
ong>&nbsp;<strong><u>Unless one can commit this amount of time, one should =
NOT take this class</u></strong>.&nbsp;<strong>In fact, if one has the choi=
ce, one should take the class during a regular 14-week
 session</strong>. My students tell me that if one takes this class during =
the summer, one must understand that one will have no life at all during th=
ese seven weeks.&nbsp;</p>
<p><span><strong>VERY IMPORTANT</strong></span>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There is an exam every week and a problem-set due at the end of the term=
. One<strong>&nbsp;must immediately download the exams and start reading th=
e notes slowly and carefully</strong>. One's goal is to finish at least the=
 a and b problems and submit them by
 the due date.<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>There is no textbook for this clas=
s</strong>. The material that one needs to read appears under Files.<span>&=
nbsp;</span><strong>All work MUST be done exactly as in the Notes with no c=
hanges, no variations, and no omissions. In
 case there are any changes, variations, or omissions, points will be deduc=
ted</strong>.&nbsp;<strong>No homework will be collected or graded. The cou=
rse grade will be based entirely on the performance on six take-home exams,=
 and a Problem Set</strong>. Please refer
 to the&nbsp;<strong>General Information Package</strong>&nbsp;under Files.=
</p>
<p><span><strong>COLLABORATION</strong></span></p>
<p><span>One</span><span>&nbsp;</span>is free to talk to anyone. But one mu=
st present one's answers in the manner in which they are presented in the N=
otes or Solved Exams with no exceptions whatsoever. Some tutors have very k=
indly made their old exams available
 to students. Some students<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>copied blindly</stron=
g><span>&nbsp;</span>from these exams in the past and got<span>&nbsp;</span=
><strong>ZEROES</strong><span>&nbsp;</span>because they could not even copy=
 faithfully and did not understand what they needed
 to change. They also adopted conventions that the current notes do not hav=
e and that are, therefore, not allowed.<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>These exa=
ms are NEW and often differ from previous exams very slightly but such slig=
ht changes may make profound differences
 in the answers</strong>. Students have individual exams that are made up f=
rom their J-numbers. Tutors will be able to read the questions in English b=
ut may not be familiar with the conventions adopted in the notes because th=
e notes did not exist in this form
 when they took the course. Every problem that one will be asked to solve h=
as an analogue in relevant notes. One must immediately start reading these =
documents as they contain exactly what I would have said in class and they =
proceed at a leisurely pace.<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>ONE
 MUST READ THE NOTES</strong>.&nbsp;<strong>Math 163 gets easier as the cou=
rse progresses</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<p><span><strong>TUTORS</strong></span></p>
<p>Dedicated tutors will be added as observers to this course. They have al=
l taken many of my classes and have been tutors for a long time for my clas=
ses. Their presence and participation are crucial to the life of this cours=
e and they will all help the current
 students.&nbsp;</p>
<p><span><strong>DISCORD GROUPS</strong></span></p>
<p>A group will be set up for each section on DISCORD and one should use th=
ese groups as one's discussion forum as the current structure of CANVAS doe=
s not allow tutors to have the required access. Currently they can observe =
but they cannot participate. But
 one may contact them within CANVAS. It may be easier to contact them on DI=
SCORD.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>To make individual appointments or to join study groups, please =
visit Tutoring and Study Groups under Files.</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;These will=
 be updated once new arrangements are announced. You may go to only these t=
utors and no one else.</p>
<p>For questions regarding tutoring, please write to Professor John Politis=
 at<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>jpolitis@ccp.edu</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>EVERY student needs to learn the language of sets and mappings</=
strong>&nbsp;because all discourse will be carried out in this language.&nb=
sp;<strong>This knowledge is required, expected, and assumed for all course=
s</strong>. This material is taught in many
 courses including Discrete Mathematics (Math 163). Therefore, even if it i=
s not required, it is a very good idea to take Discrete Mathematics. Notes =
on these topics have been uploaded.</p>
<p><strong>Please find out how to register for study groups or make individ=
ual appointments by sending an email to tutors within CANVAS or by writing =
to Professor Politis.&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Good luck.</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Sincerely,&nbsp;<br>
<br>
Atish Bagchi</p>
